/*
# Field

A labeled form row: the label, then whatever control the use site nests, and under the
control **what is wrong with it** — its `error`, once it has been judged:

    Field [ label = "Name", TextInput [ width = 220, height = 30, required = "Enter your name." ] ]

The label sits in a column beside the control (`labelWidth` is the shared-column knob), or
above it with `stacked = true`. The control reports itself to the field it is in, so the
field shows its message without being told which control it holds; a control of your own
that extends `ValueControl` does the same.

```declare
form: View [ x = 20, y = 20,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ],
    Field [ label = "Name",  TextInput [ width = 200, height = 28 ] ],
    Field [ label = "Notify", Switch [ ] ]
    ]
```

## label
The caption in the left column. It is a `Text`, not a control's own label — **a `Field`
labels whatever you nest, so the nested control's own `label` should usually be empty**
or you will render the words twice.

## baseline
The baseline a row aligns this field by — its caption's. Two fields side by side under
`align = baseline` keep their captions on one line whatever their controls' heights.

## labelWidth
The label column's width, and the knob that aligns a form. **Every row in a form sets
the same plain number** — one value, repeated or read from a shared attribute
(`labelWidth = { app.formLabels }`). It is deliberately not measured from the widest
label: aggregating over sibling rows is exactly what the constraint system refuses, so
the shared column is a value you state rather than one the form discovers.

## stacked
The label above the control rather than in a column beside it — the usual arrangement of a
form read top to bottom, and of one on a phone.

## control
The control the field holds, as it reported itself: a `TextInput` or `ValueControl` inside.
Null for anything else.

## message
What the field shows under its control: the control's `error`, empty when it says nothing.

## fieldLabel
The caption, provided to what the field holds: a `TextInput`, `Switch`, `Checkbox`, `Slider` or
`Combobox` inside takes it as its name to a screen reader unless it has its own
(`accessibilityLabel`). A control of your own reads it the same way —
`accessibilityLabel = { provided("fieldLabel", "") }`.

## _field
**Internal.** what a control inside reads to report itself to this field.

## _hold()
**Internal.** A control inside reports itself here.

## _release()
**Internal.** A control inside that goes takes itself back.
*/

class Field [
    baseline: number = { this.cap.y + this.cap.baseline },   // a row aligns the field by its caption

    label:      string = "",
    labelWidth: number = 96,
    stacked:    boolean = false,

    // the caption names the control it holds, to a screen reader as to the eye:
    // provided, and the library's value controls take it as their name

    fieldLabel: string = { label },

    // the control inside reports itself here (it reads `_field`), so the field
    // can show what is wrong with it

    _field: any = { this },
    control: any = null,
    _hold(c: any) { control = c },
    _release(c: any) { if (control === c) control = null },
    message: string = { this.control != null ? this.control.error : "" },

    layout: FieldLayout [ ],

    cap: Text [ wrap = false,
        textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted }, text = { label } ],

    msg: Text [ visible = { classroot.message != "" }, wrap = true, fontSize = 13,
        textColor = { provided("theme").error }, text = { classroot.message } ]
    ]


/*
# FieldLayout

How a `Field` arranges what it holds: the caption in its column beside the control, or
above it when the field is `stacked`; the control, or a row of them; and the message under
the control, as wide as what it holds. A `Field` makes its own; nothing else needs one.

```declare
App [ fill = white,
    Field [ x = 20, y = 20, label = "Name", stacked = true, TextInput [ width = 220, required = "Enter your name." ] ]
    ]
```
*/

class FieldLayout extends Layout [
    place() {
        const f = this.view as Field
        const kids = this.laid()
        const out = [] as any
        const x0 = f.stacked ? 0 : f.labelWidth + 12
        const y0 = f.stacked ? kids[0].height + 6 : 0
        let x = x0
        let h = 0
        for (let i = 2; i < kids.length; i++) {
            const c = kids[i]
            if (!c.visible) continue
            x = x + c.width + 12
            h = Math.max(h, c.height)
            }
        out.push(f.stacked ? ({ x: 0, y: 0 }) : ({ x: 0, y: 6, w: f.labelWidth }))
        out.push({ x: x0, y: y0 + h + 4, w: Math.max(160, x - 12 - x0) })
        let cx = x0
        for (let i = 2; i < kids.length; i++) {
            const c = kids[i]
            out.push({ x: cx, y: y0 })
            if (c.visible) cx = cx + c.width + 12
            }
        return out
        }
    ]
